{
  "chapter": "retrieval/metadata-and-authorization",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "A bug in an optimization returns worse results. A bug in an authorization control is a",
    "breach. The two look identical in the codebase.",
    "Store on the chunk what the document requires; derive from the request what the principal",
    "has. Membership is never cached in the index, because a revocation that waits for a",
    "reindex is a revocation that silently did not happen.",
    "Deny by default. A chunk with no tenant tag is invisible, not public.",
    "The principal is a required argument. There is no path that retrieves without one.",
    "The model chooses what to search for; your code decides what it is allowed to search. A",
    "tenant asked for in the query is never the tenant that is used.",
    "Post-filtering is not a security boundary. Content dropped after the search was still read",
    "out of storage and still travelled to whatever ran in between — a reranker, a log.",
    "Late binding verifies each survivor against the live source, which is the only way to",
    "close the window between an ACL change and the next ingestion.",
    "Audit at the source: every request records the principal and the document ids retrieved,",
    "because generated prose cannot be audited after the fact."
  ],
